   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits Embassy Report No. 159 surveying Japanese political, diplomatic, and economic developments during December 1939. U.S.-Japan relations centered on Ambassador Grew's conferences with Foreign Minister Nomura regarding the Japanese announcement of intent to reopen the Yangtze River to Nanking, negotiations over a possible modus vivendi prior to the expiration of the 1911 commercial treaty, and ongoing American claims regarding bombing and interference with U.S. property in China. The report also details Japan's relations with Great Britain regarding the Tientsin concession silver accord, French protests over Indochina transit demands, and the eleventh-hour renewal of the fisheries modus vivendi with the Soviet Union. Domestically, soaring prices, an acute rice shortage, and criticism of the handling of the China conflict culminated in a resolution of non-confidence by 260 Diet members against the Abe cabinet. Appended to the report are extensive monthly chronological registers of telegrams and despatches sent to the Department of State.",
