   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits Embassy Report No. 160 detailing Japanese domestic and foreign developments during January 1940. The period was dominated by the formal termination on January 26 of the 1911 U.S.-Japan Treaty of Commerce and Navigation, following which both nations enacted temporary measures to maintain non-discriminatory tariff treatment while leaving future commercial relations uncertain. The Embassy also lodged protests over Japanese aerial bombing of the Haiphong-Yunnan Railway in Chinese territory and interference with American missions and business properties. Meanwhile, relations with Great Britain reached a state of intense crisis after a British cruiser intercepted the Japanese liner Asama Maru near Tokyo on January 21 and forcibly removed twenty-one German seamen, provoking national outrage and formal protests. On the domestic front, the Abe cabinet resigned and was replaced by the Yonai cabinet amidst severe shortages of coal and electric power.",
